  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "Given a graph $G$ on $n$ vertices with $|E(G)| \\ge n^{1+\\varepsilon}$ and $\\varepsilon > 0$, Kostochka–Pyber (1988) guarantees a non-planar $H \\subseteq G$ with $|V(H)| \\le C_\\varepsilon = O_\\varepsilon(1)$. What is the time complexity of an algorithm that outputs such an $H$? Equivalently, is there an algorithm running in time $f(\\varepsilon)\\cdot\\mathrm{poly}(n)$ that returns a vertex set $S$ with $|S| \\le C_\\varepsilon$ and $G[S]$ non-planar?",
    "plain": "Kostochka and Pyber proved that any graph with n^(1+ε) edges must contain a non-planar piece on only a bounded number C_ε of vertices, regardless of how large the graph is. Their proof shows such a piece exists but does not obviously produce it efficiently. This problem asks how computationally hard it is to actually find one of these small non-planar subgraphs: can it be done in polynomial time in n for each fixed ε, or is locating the obstruction genuinely harder than knowing it exists?",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Separates existence from construction — a recurring theme (algorithmic Local Lemma, constructive Ramsey bounds) where non-constructive guarantees were later matched by efficient algorithms.",
      "Making the guarantee constructive would turn a pure existence theorem into a certified subroutine and link topological obstruction theory (Kuratowski, K5/K3,3 subdivisions) to the algorithmic theory of topological-minor testing."
    ]
  },
